  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 223 PM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nThere is good agreement through much of the extended period, with a \nheavy rainfall event and very high elevation snowfall along the \nContinental Divide. Mountain flooding and windy conditions on the \nPlains are likely.\n\nSaturday...a weak upper wave embedded in southwest flow will\nlead to showers and isolated thunderstorms along the Continental\nDivide by the afternoon. Lightning will be the main concern with\nany thunderstorms.  \n\nSaturday night through Monday...two upper waves are forecast to \ncombine across southern Colorado during this period. The first, and \nupper wave moving in off the Baja Coast is forecast to lift \nnortheast across the Desert Southwest, ahead of a secondary system \nout of the Pacific Northwest. The first upper wave will begin to \nimpact the Continental Divide Saturday night into Sunday morning. \nPWAT values in excess of 0.8 to 1.1 inches will spread north across \nColorado by Sunday morning. This will help promote heavy rainfall \nacross the San Juan Range on Sunday. Snow levels will initially be \nhigh, above 12 kft, and the tropical nature help to produce heavy \nrainfall on Sunday. There could be a few lightning strikes embedded \nover the mountains. Flooding on area streams and rivers is likely \nacross the San Juan Mountains, down into the San Luis Valley. This \nwill be monitored closely. \n\nMoving into Sunday night, the secondary wave will quickly fill in \nbehind the initial wave. This will help lower snow levels to near \n8500 feet by Monday morning. Precipitation will remain heavy across \nthe San Juan Range. For elevations above 10 kft, snow probabilities \nincrease, with 10 to 15 inches possible Sunday night into Monday. \nLower elevations will continue to moderate to periods of heavy \nrainfall, and continued flooding potential.  \n\nAcross the Plains, a cold front will arrive Monday morning, with \nvery strong winds developing behind the northerly push. Model \nguidance in general agreement with showers developing along the \nEastern Mountains, south along the Raton Mesa by late morning.\nThis activity will continue south and east through the afternoon. At \nthis time, around a quarter to half inch of rainfall is possible \nalong the lee slopes and Raton Ridge before precipitation ends \nMonday evening. Snow levels on the eastern side will remain high, \nlower to near 9 kft by Monday afternoon.\nSnow amounts of a couple inches are possible up high.  \n\nEast on the Plains, wind will be the main concern. Widespread strong \nnortherly winds are forecast to prevail Monday afternoon and night. \nGusts in excess of 40 mph will be possible, especially out near the \nKansas border. There is some guidance indicating winds could be \nstronger, approaching 50 mph. We will continue to monitor wind \nconditions as the storm system approaches.\n\nTemperatures look to remain warm both Sunday and Monday across the \nPlains. Highs in the 60s are forecast for Sunday, with around 5 to \n10 degrees cooling behind the cold front on Monday, down into the \n50s and lower 60s.  \n\nTuesday through Thursday...the upper storm system is forecast to \npark itself across eastern Kansas for mid week. While precipitation \ncome to an end Monday evening, the tight pressure gradient will keep \nwinds gusting across the Plains Tuesday and Wednesday, before upper \nlevel ridging build across southern Colorado on Thursday. \nTemperatures will remain cool, with mostly 50s across the lower \nelevations. Winds with gusts 30 to 40 mph will remain possible on \nthe Plains Tuesday and Wednesday, decreasing on Thursday.",
